WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.270
Why am i attracted to trying to solve
simple problems. Well I think one reason

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.300
is that I've always been attracted to
carnival games and you know I like

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:13.460
looking at these things like trying to
throw a ring around a coke bottle or

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.830
walk a tightrope or you know bowl
over glasses with the ball and you know

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:22.820
just thinking about how can you do this
these kind of very simple looking things

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.520
anyone can understand what you're
supposed to do but then it's very hard

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.020
to do it and so I've always just liked
the kind of the mental challenge of

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:33.950
doing things like that I also like
simple problems because I think that

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:38.059
they reveal hidden depths in mathematics
if there's some problem that's been

00:00:38.059 --> 00:00:41.300
around for a long time and no one's able
to solve it it means that somehow

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.600
there's a kind of completely new idea
that you need to solve it so there's

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.340
this great appeal of trying to come up
with some new idea that no one's ever

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.300
seen before
a third thing is that I like to do

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.680
experiments and these kind of simple
looking problems like the one on my

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.550
screen here seeing is called Langton
Zandt this is a famous unsolved problem

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:08.240
in maybe in complex systems or in
dynamics so I like to I like to make

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.509
computer programs and I like to run
experiments and kind of gather data and

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:15.859
see if I can notice patterns and these
simple problems are kind of you know

00:01:15.859 --> 00:01:21.139
grist for the mill I can just just code
them up pretty quickly and I can do it

00:01:21.139 --> 00:01:26.929
um another thing I like about simple
problems is I'm sort of lazy about

00:01:26.929 --> 00:01:31.520
learning new stuff and a very impatient
person so I like to be able to just hear

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.259
the problem and just kind of jump right
in and start thinking about it I think

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:43.039
of these kind of simple problems is sort
of like Lords of giant mansions and and

00:01:43.039 --> 00:01:46.159
they're kind of they're willing to sort
of let you into the ante room they'll

00:01:46.159 --> 00:01:48.829
kind of you can kind of walk around the
lobby and they might even kind of let

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:52.929
you up on the first floor so they
started accessible in a certain sense

00:01:52.929 --> 00:01:56.389
and then there's always a hope that
there's like some back door into the

00:01:56.389 --> 00:01:59.739
mansion you can go all the way up

